  "sections": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "content": "One of the trickiest things for English speakers learning Catalan (and Spanish) is that there are two separate verbs that both translate as 'to be': 'ser' and 'estar'. The choice between them is not random - it follows clear rules."
    },
    {
      "type": "rule",
      "content": "Use SER for permanent or defining characteristics: identity, origin, material, time, and inherent qualities."
    },
    {
      "type": "examples",
      "items": [
        {
          "catalan": "Soc anglès",
          "english": "I am English",
          "note": "origin/identity - ser"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "Soc de Londres",
          "english": "I am from London",
          "note": "origin - ser"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "És metge",
          "english": "He/she is a doctor",
          "note": "profession - ser"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "És professora",
          "english": "She is a teacher",
          "note": "profession - ser"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "Avui és dilluns",
          "english": "Today is Monday",
          "note": "time - ser"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "La taula és de fusta",
          "english": "The table is made of wood",
          "note": "material - ser"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "Ets molt alt",
          "english": "You are very tall",
          "note": "inherent physical quality - ser"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "rule",
      "content": "Use ESTAR for temporary states and conditions that can change: how someone feels, how something is right now."
    },
    {
      "type": "examples",
      "items": [
        {
          "catalan": "Estic cansat",
          "english": "I am tired",
          "note": "temporary state - estar"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "Estic bé, gràcies",
          "english": "I'm fine, thanks",
          "note": "how you feel - estar"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "El restaurant està tancat",
          "english": "The restaurant is closed",
          "note": "temporary state - estar"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "Com estàs?",
          "english": "How are you?",
          "note": "current state - estar (you know this from the starter deck!)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "rule",
      "content": "For WHERE something or someone is, Catalan normally uses SER + a: L'estació és a prop. With people, estar is also common: Estem a Barcelona."
    },
    {
      "type": "examples",
      "items": [
        {
          "catalan": "On és l'estació?",
          "english": "Where is the station?",
          "note": "location of a place - ser"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "El bany és al fons",
          "english": "The toilet is at the back",
          "note": "location of a place - ser"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "On ets?",
          "english": "Where are you?",
          "note": "location of a person - ser"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "Som a Barcelona / Estem a Barcelona",
          "english": "We are in Barcelona",
          "note": "people: both are fine"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "content": "Here are the present tense conjugations of both verbs - you'll need them constantly."
    },
    {
      "type": "examples",
      "items": [
        {
          "catalan": "jo soc / jo estic",
          "english": "I am",
          "note": "ser / estar"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "tu ets / tu estàs",
          "english": "you are (informal)",
          "note": "ser / estar"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "ell/ella és / ell/ella està",
          "english": "he/she is",
          "note": "ser / estar"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "nosaltres som / nosaltres estem",
          "english": "we are",
          "note": "ser / estar"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "vosaltres sou / vosaltres esteu",
          "english": "you are (plural)",
          "note": "ser / estar"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "ells/elles són / ells/elles estan",
          "english": "they are",
          "note": "ser / estar"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "practice": [
    {
      "id": "se-p01",
      "kind": "multiple_choice",
      "question": "I am from London. Which is correct?",
      "options": [
        "Soc de Londres (ser)",
        "Estic de Londres (estar)"
      ],
      "answer": "Soc de Londres (ser)",
      "explanation": "Origin → ser."
    },
    {
      "id": "se-p02",
      "kind": "multiple_choice",
      "question": "I'm fine, thanks. Which is correct?",
      "options": [
        "Soc bé, gràcies (ser)",
        "Estic bé, gràcies (estar)"
      ],
      "answer": "Estic bé, gràcies (estar)",
      "explanation": "How you feel → estar."
    },
    {
      "id": "se-p03",
      "kind": "multiple_choice",
      "question": "Where is the station? Which is correct?",
      "options": [
        "On és l'estació? (ser)",
        "On està l'estació? (estar)"
      ],
      "answer": "On és l'estació? (ser)",
      "explanation": "Where a place is → ser + a. 'On està...?' is common in speech, but 'On és...?' is the standard form."
    },
    {
      "id": "se-p04",
      "kind": "multiple_choice",
      "question": "She is a teacher. Which is correct?",
      "options": [
        "És professora (ser)",
        "Està professora (estar)"
      ],
      "answer": "És professora (ser)",
      "explanation": "Profession → ser."
    },
